\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 The If\u8230?Then\u8230?ElseIf Statement (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The general format of the Block If statement:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If <condition> Then\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (condition is true - do all of the actions\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab coded in this branch)\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Else\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (condition is false - do all of the actions\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab coded in this branch)\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab End If\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 7\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 The If\u8230?Then\u8230?Else Statement\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If the expression is True\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Execute the statements between If\u8230?Then and Else\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If the expression is False\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Execute the statements between Else and End If\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 expression}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 statement}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 (}{\cf5 more}{\cf5  }{\cf5 statements}{\cf5  }{\cf5 may}{\cf5  }{\cf5 follow}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf2 Else}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 statement}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 (}{\cf5 more}{\cf5  }{\cf5 statements}{\cf5  }{\cf5 may}{\cf5  }{\cf5 follow}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 8\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 The If\u8230?Then\u8230?Else Statement (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Learn these rules:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A block If statement must always end with an End If statement.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The key word Then must appear on the same line as the word If.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The key words Else and End If must appear on lines by themselves.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The Else branch is optional (see example below).   Only use an Else statement if there is a False branch.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Visual Basic automatically indents 4 spaces inside the True and False branches to make the statement easier to read.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 9\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Relational Operators\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Usually a condition is formed using a relational operator\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A relational operator determines if a specific relationship exists between two values\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 >}{\cf5         }{\cf5 Greater}{\cf5  }{\cf5 than}\line {\cf5 <}{\cf5         }{\cf5 Less}{\cf5  }{\cf5 than}\line {\cf5 =}{\cf5         }{\cf5 Equal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 to}\line {\cf5 <}{\cf5 >}{\cf5               }{\cf2 Not}{\cf5  }{\cf5 equal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 to}\line {\cf5 >}{\cf5 =}{\cf5               }{\cf5 Greater}{\cf5  }{\cf5 than}{\cf5  }{\cf2 or}{\cf5  }{\cf5 equal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 to}\line {\cf5 <}{\cf5 =}{\cf5               }{\cf5 Less}{\cf5  }{\cf5 than}{\cf5  }{\cf2 or}{\cf5  }{\cf5 equal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 to}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 10\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Boolean Expressions\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Relational operators are binary \u8211? meaning they use two operands, for example:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab length > width    Is length greater than width?\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab size <= 10        Is size less than or equal 10?\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Relational operators are used in Boolean expressions which yield a True or False result\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab IF GRD >65.4 OR GRD < 69.5 then GRD = 2.75\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 11\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Putting It All Together\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If\u8230?Then statement examples:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 decSales}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 50000}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 MessageBox}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Show}{\cf5 (}{\cf3 "You've earned a bonus!"}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 decSales}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 50000}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 MessageBox}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Show}{\cf5 (}{\cf3 "You've earned a bonus!"}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 decCommissionRate}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf6 0.12}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 intDaysOff}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intDaysOff}{\cf5  }{\cf5 +}{\cf5  }{\cf6 1}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 12\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Using Relational Operators with Math Operators\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Math operators are evaluated before relational operators\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab intX + intY and intA - intB are evaluated first\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Most programmers prefer to use parentheses to clarify the order of operations\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intX}{\cf5  }{\cf5 +}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intY}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intA}{\cf5  }{\cf5 -}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intB}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "It is true!"}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 (}{\cf5 intX}{\cf5  }{\cf5 +}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intY}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf5 (}{\cf5 intA}{\cf5  }{\cf5 \u8211?}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intB}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "It is true!"}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 13\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Using Function Calls with Relational Operators\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Either or both relational operator operands may be function calls\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The return value of the function call is compared to the value using the relational operator\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf2 CInt}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 txtInput}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf5 <}{\cf5  }{\cf6 100}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "It is true!"}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 14\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Using Boolean Variables as Flags\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A flag is a Boolean variable that signals when some condition exists in the program\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Since a Boolean variable is either True or False, it can be used as the condition of an If\u8230?Then statement\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Since a Boolean variable already evaluates to True or False, an = operator is not required\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 blnQuotaMet}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "You have met your sales quota"}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 15\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Nested If Statements\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If Statements Within If Statements\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Any type of statement may be used inside a set of Then, Else, or ElseIf statements of an If\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 This}{\cf5  }{\cf5 includes}{\cf5  }{\cf5 other}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 statements}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If statements within If statements create a more complex decision structure called a    Nested If\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab IF(AH17="","",IF(AH17>=97.5,1,IF(AH17>=94.5,1.25,IF(AH17>=91.5,1.5,IF(AH17>=88.5,1.75,IF(AH17>=85.5,2,IF(AH17>=82.5,2.25,IF(AH17>=97.5,2.5,IF(AH17>=76.5,2.75,IF(AH17>=74.5,3,5))))))))))\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 16\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Examining the Nested If Statement\b0\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 dblSalary}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 30000}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intYearsOnJob}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 2}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5         }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Applicant qualifies."}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 Else}\line {\cf5         }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Applicant does not qualify."}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\line {\cf2 Else}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intYearsOnJob}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 5}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5         }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Applicant qualifies."}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 Else}\line {\cf5         }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Applicant does not qualify."}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
