\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The ToLower method can be applied to a string\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Results in a string with uppercase letters converted to lowercase\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The original string is not changed\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab General Format:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab In the following example, strLittleTown is assigned the string "new york" using the ToLower method:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 StringExpression}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 ToUpper}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 strLittleWord}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Hello"}\line {\cf5 strBigWord}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strLittleWord}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 ToUpper}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 StringExpression}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 ToLower}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 strBigTown}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "NEW YORK"}\line {\cf5 strLittleTown}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strBigTown}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 ToLower}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 38\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 The IsNumeric Function\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab This function accepts a string as an argument and returns True if the string contains a number\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Use IsNumeric function to determine if a given string contains numeric data\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strNumber}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}\line {\cf5 strNumber}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "576"}\line {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 IsNumeric}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 strNumber}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf4 ' Returns true}\line {\cf5 strNumber}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "123abc"}\line {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 IsNumeric}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 strNumber}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf4 ' Returns false}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 39\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Determining the Length of a String\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The Length property, a member of the String class, returns the number of characters in a string\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab In the following example, the intNumChars variable contains the value 6:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab You can also determine the length of a control's Text property, as shown in the following code:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strName}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Herman"}\line {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 intNumChars}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Integer}\line {\cf5 intNumChars}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strName}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Length}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 If}{\cf5  }{\cf5 txtInput}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Length}{\cf5  }{\cf5 >}{\cf5  }{\cf6 20}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Then}\line {\cf5     }{\cf5 lblMessage}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Please enter no more than 20 characters."}\line {\cf2 End}{\cf5  }{\cf2 If}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 40\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Trimming Spaces from Strings\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab There are three methods that remove spaces from strings:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 TrimStart}{\cf5   }{\cf5 -}{\cf5  }{\cf5 removes}{\cf5  }{\cf5 leading}{\cf5  }{\cf5 spaces}\line {\cf5 TrimEnd}{\cf5       }{\cf5 -}{\cf5  }{\cf5 removes}{\cf5  }{\cf5 trailing}{\cf5  }{\cf5 spaces}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Trim             - removes leading and trailing spaces\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Here is the general format for each method:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab An example with three leading and trailing spaces, using each method:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 StringExpression}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 TrimStart}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf5 StringExpression}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 TrimEnd}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\line {\cf5 StringExpression}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Trim}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf5 strGreeting}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "    Hello    "}\line {\cf5 lblMessage1}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strGreeting}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 TrimStart}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}{\cf5  }{\cf4 ' "Hello    "}\line {\cf5 lblMessage2}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strGreeting}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 TrimEnd}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}{\cf5    }{\cf4 ' "    Hello"}\line {\cf5 lblMessage3}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Text}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 strGreeting}{\cf5 .}{\cf5 Trim}{\cf5 (}{\cf5 )}{\cf5       }{\cf4 ' "Hello"}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 41\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Looping Statement\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A repetition structure, or loop causes one or more statements to repeat\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Each repetition of the loop is called an iteration\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Visual Basic has three types of loops:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Do}{\cf5  }{\cf2 While}{\cf5     }{\cf5 variable}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf5 value}\line {\cf5     }{\cf2 Do}{\cf5  }{\cf5 Until}\line {\cf5         }{\cf2 For}{\cf5 \u8230?}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Next}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The difference among them is how they control the repetition\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 42\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Looping Statement (cont.)\b0\par
